1. d4 {overall lesson: don't just blindly play his worst database opening if you have never played it/studied it properly, I spent 10 minutes looking through lines vaguely before the game and it cost me. Keep prepping with databases but stick to structures/openings that I know well, the aim isi to get a good position out the opening, not just crush him straight away

&nbsp;} Nf6  (1... a6  ) 2. c4 c5 3. d5 b5 4. cxb5 a6 5. bxa6 g6  (5... d6  6. e4 Bxa6 7. Bxa6 Nxa6 8. Ne2) 6. a7 Rxa7 7. Nc3 Bg7 8. e4 d6  (8... O-O  9. e5) 9. Nf3 {out of the opening, I'm down a pawn and with no clear compensation yet. my rook is misplaced} O-O 10. Be2 Qa5?! {better was to strike in the centre immediately with e6, a common idea in the benko gambit.}  (10... e6!  11. dxe6 Bxe6 {[%cal Ge6a2,Gg7b2,Gb8a6,Ga6b4]yes the d6 pawn is a weakness, but I should have evaluated the resulting pressure on the queenside was worth it, which is the whole point of the benko gambit}) 11. Bd2 Qb6 12. Qc2 Bg4?? {In my head I thought I was being clever, breaking rules by giving away the bishop pair for knight, but I should have realised that white knight is actually bad at the moment and is restricted by my pawns. the bishop on d2 is also blocking it from getting to C for. The better plan was to go ba6 and trade off his light squared bishop. My whole idea was to take and then put a knight on e5, but I should have realised this is the type of position a bishop pair really thrives. My pressure against white's queenside is simply not enough/almost non existent}  (12... Ba6! {best was to pressure the light squared diagonal, if white takes the my knight comes in with tempo which helps me. }  13. Bxa6  (13. O-O  Rb7 14. Na4 Qa7 15. Nc3)  (13. a4  Bxe2 14. Nxe2  (14. Kxe2  e6) Na6) Qxa6) 13. O-O  (13. h3  ) Bxf3?? 14. Bxf3 Nfd7 15. Be2 Na6 16. Nb5 {now we see the point of a7, now nb7 comes with tempo} Raa8 17. Bc3 Ne5 18. a4 Rfc8 19. f4 Nb4  (19... Nd7  20. Bxg7 Kxg7 21. Qc3+ f6 22. Kh1) 20. Qd2 Nd7 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Qc3+ f6 23. Kh1  (23. Qh3  Rd8 24. Qe6 Kf8 25. e5 dxe5 26. d6 Kg7  (26... Re8  27. Qxd7  (27. Bc4  Kg7 28. Qxd7 Nc2 29. fxe5 f5 30. Nc7 Qxb2 31. Nxe8+ Rxe8 32. Qxe8 Ne3 33. Qxe7+ Kh6 34. Qh4+ Kg7 35. Qf2 Qc3 36. Qxe3 Qxe3+ 37. Kh1))  (26... exd6  )) Qb8 24. Qh3 Rd8  (24... Qb7  25. Bg4) 25. Qe6 Kf8 26. e5 dxe5 27. d6 Re8 28. Bc4  (28. Qxd7  ) Kg7 29. Qxd7 Nc2 30. N